From 340fafa6d2e6bf6808eb47630b869c837cbd8df1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 17:21:57 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 099/106] tls: go on with another uTLS fingerprint when the
 ClientHello gets no acknowledgment

A middlebox that judges a connection by its ClientHello may drop the ClientHello of
one browser and pass that of another. The TCP connection is made, the ClientHello is
sent and never acknowledged, and the handshake waits out its timeout on every
connection.

With SING_BOX_UTLS_FALLBACK set to a list of fingerprints, the TLS dialer watches the
connection while a handshake with a uTLS fingerprint runs (TCP_INFO, Linux, a TCP
socket of the kernel). When what the client wrote stays unacknowledged through two
retransmissions, nothing having come from the server, the connection is given up and
dialed again with the next fingerprint: the configured one first, then the list, three
handshakes a dial at most. The fingerprint that got through is used from then on for
the local address it worked from; when it stops being answered, the list goes on. For
REALITY the fingerprints it refuses, or accepts only from older servers, are left
out. With tls.spoof nothing is remembered, and the report names it as a possible
cause. Each step is logged.

Without the variable nothing changes, and a name in it that is not a fingerprint
turns the fallback off with a warning.

Measured on the loopback with nftables dropping the ClientHello of chrome on its way
in: the first dial gets through with the next fingerprint in 0.85 s, the next ones
begin with it; without the list the handshake hangs until its timeout.
